## Background and Motivation

`Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform` (#69304) gives Blazor applications typed access to standard browser APIs. `Window.Screen` (#69969) reads the size and the color depth of the screen. But an application that changes its layout or behavior when the user rotates the device cannot read the orientation, and it cannot learn when the orientation changes. Today it needs `IJSRuntime` and its own JavaScript for that.

This proposal adds `screen.orientation`, the last part of the browser context family (#69872) that #69969 left for later. It covers the `type` and `angle` attributes of `ScreenOrientation` and its `change` event. The event uses the event listener API from #69922, as the `change` event of Cookie Store does.

`lock()` and `unlock()` are not part of this proposal. They are not Baseline, and Safari does not support them.

The implementation is in #70562.

## Usage Examples

```csharp
builder.Services.AddBrowserPlatform();
```

```razor
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject IBrowserPlatform Browser

<div class="@(portrait ? "stacked" : "side-by-side")">
    ...
</div>

@code {
    private bool portrait;
    private IAsyncDisposable? changeListener;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            var orientation = Browser.Window.Screen.Orientation;
            changeListener = await orientation.AddEventListenerAsync(ScreenOrientation.ChangeEvent, _ => UpdateAsync());
            await UpdateAsync();
        }
    }

    private async Task UpdateAsync()
    {
        var type = await Browser.Window.Screen.Orientation.GetTypeAsync();
        portrait = type == OrientationType.PortraitPrimary || type == OrientationType.PortraitSecondary;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        if (changeListener is not null)
        {
            await changeListener.DisposeAsync();
        }
    }
}
```

The API works in Interactive Server and WebAssembly.

## Proposed API

```diff
 namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform;

+public sealed class ScreenOrientation
+{
+    public static EventType<ScreenOrientation, Event> ChangeEvent { get; }
+    public ValueTask<OrientationType> GetTypeAsync();
+    public ValueTask<ushort> GetAngleAsync();
+    public ValueTask<IAsyncDisposable> AddEventListenerAsync<TEvent>(EventType<ScreenOrientation, TEvent> type, Func<TEvent, Task> listener);
+    public ValueTask<IAsyncDisposable> AddEventListenerAsync<TEvent>(EventType<ScreenOrientation, TEvent> type, Func<TEvent, Task> listener, CancellationToken cancellationToken);
+}
+
+public readonly struct OrientationType : IEquatable<OrientationType>
+{
+    public OrientationType(string value);
+    public static OrientationType PortraitPrimary { get; }
+    public static OrientationType PortraitSecondary { get; }
+    public static OrientationType LandscapePrimary { get; }
+    public static OrientationType LandscapeSecondary { get; }
+    public string Value { get; }
+    public bool Equals(OrientationType other);
+    public override bool Equals(object? obj);
+    public override int GetHashCode();
+    public override string ToString();
+    public static bool operator ==(OrientationType left, OrientationType right);
+    public static bool operator !=(OrientationType left, OrientationType right);
+}
+
+public sealed class Event
+{
+    public Event();
+}
+
+public static class ScreenScreenOrientationExtensions
+{
+    extension(Screen screen)
+    {
+        public ScreenOrientation Orientation { get; }
+    }
+}
```

The names and types follow the Web IDL of [Screen Orientation](https://github.com/w3c/webref/blob/main/ed/idl/screen-orientation.idl). `unsigned short` becomes `ushort`.

`screen.orientation` is `[SameObject]`, so `Screen.Orientation` is a synchronous property, like `Window.Screen`. Every caller gets the same instance, and the DI scope disposes it. The orientation changes while the page is open, so each method reads the current value from the browser.

`OrientationType` is a `readonly struct` that wraps the Web IDL string, not an `enum`. This is the rule for every Web IDL enum in the package (#69991), as for `CookieSameSite`. A value that a newer browser returns is kept as it is, even if the type does not define it. Unlike `CookieSameSite`, the Web IDL gives `OrientationType` no default. So `default(OrientationType)` holds no string. Its `Value` throws `InvalidOperationException`, and its `ToString()` returns an empty string.

The `change` event has one key, `ScreenOrientation.ChangeEvent`, as #69922 describes. `ScreenOrientation` gets the two `AddEventListenerAsync` overloads that `CookieStore` has.

The `change` event is a plain `Event` in the specification, with no members of its own. #70529 (the `online` and `offline` events) proposes the same empty `Event` class for such events. This proposal adds the same type on purpose, so that the two proposals and their PRs can be reviewed and merged in either order. The PR that merges second will take the type from the first. `Event` is listed here only so that this proposal is complete on its own.

The extension class is named `<Owner><Type>Extensions`, after the type that the property returns, as the porting skill says. So it is `ScreenScreenOrientationExtensions`.

A browser without `screen.orientation`, such as Safari before 16.4, makes every method throw `NotSupportedException`, as Cookie Store does when the browser has no `cookieStore`.

## Alternative Designs

Applications can keep using `IJSRuntime` with their own JavaScript.

#69969 showed `OrientationType` as a C# `enum`. An `enum` cannot hold a value that a newer browser adds, so #69991 chose the struct for all Web IDL enums.

#69969 also named the extension class `ScreenOrientationExtensions`. That name reads well, but it breaks the naming rule that every other family follows, so this proposal uses `ScreenScreenOrientationExtensions`.

`ScreenOrientation` could read `type` and `angle` in one call and return a snapshot. That would save one round trip in Interactive Server. But a snapshot is not the browser API, as #69969 said for `Screen`.

The `change` event could have its own event type, such as `ScreenOrientationChangeEvent`. The specification fires a plain `Event`, so it uses the shared `Event` from #70529.

## Risks

`GetTypeAsync()` sits next to `object.GetType()`. It follows the rule that a readable attribute `foo` becomes `GetFooAsync()`, and the return type tells the two apart.

`Event` is a general name in the `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform` namespace, as #70529 says. It is the Web IDL name, as `StorageEvent` and `CookieChangeEvent` are.

Like the other families of the package, the API is unavailable before interactivity.

## Source Justifications

- The screen orientation is part of the browser context family: "Screen and screen orientation." (#69872)
- Applications read browser information to adapt their behavior: "Expose browser environment information needed by applications to adapt their behavior." (#69872)
- The Epic asks for the reaction to a rotated device without polling: "The UI updates by itself when the user changes a setting in another tab, goes offline, or rotates the device. The app does not poll." (#69987)
- The orientation was left out of the Screen proposal for later: "#69872 also lists the screen orientation. It will follow in a later PR, after #69968 (Navigator) is merged." (#69969)
- The orientation is an object below `Screen` that returns the same object: "`[SameObject] readonly attribute ScreenOrientation orientation;`" ([screen-orientation.idl](https://github.com/w3c/webref/blob/main/ed/idl/screen-orientation.idl))
- The members and their types: "`readonly attribute OrientationType type;`", "`readonly attribute unsigned short angle;`", "`attribute EventHandler onchange;`" ([screen-orientation.idl](https://github.com/w3c/webref/blob/main/ed/idl/screen-orientation.idl))
- The values of `OrientationType`: "`"portrait-primary"`", "`"portrait-secondary"`", "`"landscape-primary"`", "`"landscape-secondary"`" ([screen-orientation.idl](https://github.com/w3c/webref/blob/main/ed/idl/screen-orientation.idl))
- The feature is for adapting the layout: "With this API, you can adapt an application's layout or behavior in response to changes in orientation." (`screen-orientation` in [web-features](https://github.com/web-platform-dx/web-features))
- `type`, `angle`, and the `change` event are Baseline widely available: "`baseline: high`", "`baseline_high_date: 2025-09-27`", "`api.ScreenOrientation.change_event`" (`screen-orientation` in [web-features](https://github.com/web-platform-dx/web-features))
- Older Safari has no `screen.orientation`: "`safari: "16.4"`" (`screen-orientation` in [web-features](https://github.com/web-platform-dx/web-features))
- `lock()` and `unlock()` are not Baseline: "`baseline: false`" (`screen-orientation-lock` in [web-features](https://github.com/web-platform-dx/web-features))
- Every Web IDL enum is a struct: "Map every Web IDL enum to a hand-written `readonly struct` that wraps the string, whether the application passes it or the browser returns it." (`.github/skills/port-components-platform-api/references/type-mapping.md`)
- The rule for an enum without a default: "The constructor stores the string as it is, so the field is `null` only in `default(T)`. `Value` throws for it, but `ToString()` must not" (`.github/skills/port-components-platform-api/references/type-mapping.md`)
- The name of the extension class: "Name the extension class `<Owner><Type>Extensions` after the type that the property returns" (`.github/skills/port-components-platform-api/SKILL.md`)
- The event key: "Give each event one key, a static `EventType<TTarget, TEvent>` property named `<Name>Event`, on the target type" (`.github/skills/port-components-platform-api/SKILL.md`)
- A target other than `Window` gets its own listener methods: "When the target is not `Window`, give it two `AddEventListenerAsync<TEvent>` overloads, one with a `CancellationToken` and one without" (`.github/skills/port-components-platform-api/SKILL.md`)
- The same `Event` type for the `online` and `offline` events: "`Event` is the .NET type for such an event, and it has no members for now." (#70529)
- The event object copies nothing: "`EventTypes.Create<ScreenOrientation, Event>("change", "[{}]")`" (`ScreenOrientation.cs`)
- A missing `screen.orientation` throws `NotSupportedException`: "`reference = await _jsRuntime.GetValueAsync<IJSObjectReference?>("screen.orientation").ConfigureAwait(false);`", "`throw new NotSupportedException(NotAvailableMessage, ex);`" (`ScreenOrientation.cs`)
- The API works in Interactive Server and WebAssembly: "`ScreenPaths = ["/screen-server", "/screen-wasm"]`" (`ScreenTests.cs`)

